S157 LRC14 FOURIER-TOEPLITZ PSD DUAL
==============================================================================
max_degree=512, negative_tol=1e-08, single_limit=180, two_swap_limit=36, alias_depth=4, lcm_tail_max=5
[0] Assumption challenge / quotient declaration
  considered vertices:
    runners, danger arcs, exact endpoints, safe components, count states,
    Fourier modes, Toeplitz rows, Fejer centers, denominator ladders,
    endpoint owners, packet labels, and proof obligations.
  chosen vertices:
    Fourier modes and explicit Toeplitz PSD-test vectors.
  preserved LRC predicate:
    a strict counterexample requires F_S=C_S-1>=0 a.e.; any negative
    Toeplitz quadratic form contradicts that and proves a strict safe set.
  destroyed information:
    endpoint-owner labels and exact interval atlas, except for the midpoint
    used to place the Fejer vector.
  challenged assumption:
    HYP-2973's danger-count distribution may not be the only moment
    object; phase-sensitive Fourier fibers can certify rows while retaining
    divisor/Farey structure.

[1] Fourier-Toeplitz necessary condition
  Define C_S(t)=#{v in S : ||v*t||<1/14} and F_S=C_S-1.
  A strict counterexample would have F_S(t)>=0 almost everywhere.
  Hence every Toeplitz moment matrix [hat F_S(i-j)] must be PSD.
  A single vector p with p^*T_d p<0 is a dual certificate for a
  strict safe interval.

  Divisor-curried Fourier coefficients:
    c_0 = |S|/7 - 1 = 6/7 for 13-speed LRC14 rows
    c_k = sum_{v|k, v in S} sin(pi*(k/v)/7)/(pi*(k/v)), k>0
  Low modes only see the speed divisor fibers.  This is the
  phase-sensitive cousin of HYP-2973's count distribution.

  Fejer vector used here:
    p_j=exp(-2*pi*i*j*x)/sqrt(d+1), centered at a safe-component midpoint.
    Q_d(x)=c_0+2*sum_{k<=d}(1-k/(d+1))*c_k*cos(2*pi*k*x).
    Q_d(x)<0 proves T_d is not PSD, but may occur after the true first
    negative eigenvalue.  Floating margins are reported; exact interval
    enclosures are the next formalization step.

